{"author":{"id":"f4e4dbe251fbd7519eab696986bae317e93a786739d6cebe9c4c1e89dd44e465"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793683058,"nonce":"hLHFB3rCyVymkZM5A4mjWVYd1oU=","message":"Milestone: M6 (`9002a2c212f7349c24dda905b33ed163c03e32e96b5d052ea82bc35c00e36618`).\n\n- M6 adds exceptions, `null`, `/` and `%`, `checked` overflow and the effect monad. These are where the model and the CLR most easily disagree: which of two failing operations throws first, `int.MinValue / -1`, `checked` and `unchecked` blocks nested inside each other, and `null` reaching code through record fields. Random programs that mix them find mismatches that a hand-written corpus misses.\n- Before M6 the subset is small: no division, exceptions or loops. The hand-written corpus plus property-based inputs (`624cb3c`, M5a) cover much of it.\n- It reuses `624cb3c`'s shrinking and replay, to reduce a failing program to a minimal one. Extend the generator in M7 for loops and the heap model.\n\nNear-miss declarations, which the subset must reject by name, are included here and not split out.","files":null}]}